Every housekeeping department has a checklist. Most are good. The problem is not what is on them; it is what they cannot do. A static **housekeeping SOP checklist** tells an attendant what to do, but it cannot tell a supervisor, a front desk or an auditor whether the room is actually ready.

This article gives you a complete guest room reset checklist you can adapt, then shows how to turn it into verified room readiness: a timed task that proves itself. For the product view, see the [housekeeping SOP app](/solutions/housekeeping).

## Checklist vs verified room readiness

The difference is evidence.

- A **checklist** is a list of steps. Ticking it proves only that someone ticked it.
- **Verified room readiness** means the room was done to a specific standard version, by a named person, within a known time, with photo evidence on the details that matter and a supervisor's sign-off before release.

The first is a reminder. The second is a record.

## A complete housekeeping SOP checklist for guest room reset

Use this as a starting point and adapt it to your brand standard and room types. It is written the way it should run: one action per step, in four phases.

### Prepare

1. Knock, announce and check the room status before entering.
2. Ventilate the room and switch on lights.
3. Strip the bed and remove used linen and towels.
4. Remove rubbish and used amenities; note any lost property.

### Perform

5. Clean and sanitise the toilet, shower and basin.
6. Polish the vanity, mirror and fittings to remove water marks.
7. Replace towels and bathroom amenities to par.
8. Make the bed to brand standard, with the runner centred.
9. Dust and polish surfaces, including the headboard and bedside tables.
10. Restock in-room amenities, stationery and minibar to par.
11. Vacuum the floor, including under the bed and behind the door.

### Verify

12. Check all lights, the television, air conditioning and safe.
13. Guest-eye scan: view the room from the doorway and the bed.
14. Flag any maintenance issue for engineering.

### Release

15. Release the room to the front office.

## Turn the checklist into verified room readiness

### 1. Give the room a target time

Time a few competent attendants doing the checklist as written and use a figure close to the median. A target time makes the standard plannable, and it shows you which rooms or room types consistently take longer. Mise shows the attendant a live countdown against it, and the manager dashboard shows drift across the floor.

### 2. Add reference photos

Attach photos of the finished bathroom vanity, the made bed and the amenity tray. They remove most "is this right?" questions, which is how you start to reduce the [supervisor bottleneck](/problems/supervisor-bottleneck).

### 3. Gate the steps guests notice

Choose two or three steps that must produce a photo before they can be ticked. For a guest room reset, the strongest candidates are step 6 (vanity and fittings), step 8 (bed and runner) and step 13 (guest-eye scan). These [photo gates](/glossary/photo-gate) make evidence a requirement of the work, not a favour.

Resist gating everything. Fifteen photos per room slows the work and buries the evidence that matters.

### 4. Require sign-off before release

Decide whether a supervisor must sign off before the room is released. With photo evidence attached, many sign-offs can happen from the manager dashboard without a walk. The sign-off is recorded in the task with the supervisor's name and time.

### 5. Let the record do the reporting

Every completed room writes an entry to the [service record](/glossary/service-record): standard and version, attendant, room, start and finish time against target, steps, photos and sign-off. Nobody fills in a sheet at the end of the shift.

## The same checklist, before and after

| | Static checklist | Verified room readiness |
|---|---|---|
| Where it lives | Laminated sheet or clipboard | The attendant's phone, as a timed task |
| Time | Not tracked | Target time with live countdown |
| What done looks like | Words | Reference photos |
| Proof | A tick | Photo gates, timestamps, sign-off |
| Release | Radio call to the desk | Recorded release in the task |
| Audit value | Low | A filterable, attributable record |

## Adapting the checklist to other room states

A guest room reset after departure is the most demanding version of the checklist, but it is not the only one. Keep one standard per room state rather than one giant checklist with optional steps, because optional steps are the first to be skipped.

| Room state | What changes from the full reset | Suggested target | Suggested photo gates |
|---|---|---|---|
| Departure (full reset) | All fifteen steps | Your measured median | Vanity, bed, guest-eye scan |
| Stayover | No bed strip; linen per guest preference; respect guest belongings | Shorter than departure | Bathroom finish |
| VIP arrival | Full reset plus amenity setup and a second verification | Longer than departure | Amenity setup, guest-eye scan |
| Turndown | Bed turned down, lights set, amenities placed, curtains drawn | Short | Bed and amenity placement |
| Out of order | Maintenance flag, room blocked, engineering notified | N/A | Photo of the fault |

Each becomes its own timed task with its own target time and gates. The attendant only ever sees the steps that apply to the room in front of them, and the service record shows exactly which standard ran in which room. That separation also makes the data useful: stayover times and departure times answer different staffing questions, and mixing them hides both.

Remember to version each standard separately. When the turndown standard changes, you want the record to show which rooms were done to the new version from which date.

## A room inspection checklist for supervisors

Supervisors still inspect in person where it matters: VIP arrivals, rooms with repeated issues, new joiners' early rooms. When they do, the inspection should use the same standard and be recorded against the same task. A useful supervisor pass:

- Compare the gated photos with the reference photos.
- Check the three details guests most often mention in reviews at your property.
- Confirm maintenance flags were raised, not just noticed.
- Sign off in the task, or return the room with a note.

## Where to start

Start with one room type on one floor for a week. Compare the service record with what your current inspection sheets show. Most teams find the conversation changes quickly: from "did you do the room properly?" to "the vanity photo on 208 shows water marks; let's look at the method."

When the checklist is running, the next step is the rest of the department. Our guide to [digitizing hotel SOPs](/digital-sop) covers which standards to convert next, and [hotel audit readiness](/audit-readiness) shows how the same record answers your next audit.
